WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.509
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.509 --> 00:00:09.830
In the series, we talk a lot about photorealistic
rendering and making sure that the appearance

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:13.050
of our virtual objects is simulated properly.

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:15.179
A lot of works on how things look.

00:00:15.179 --> 00:00:20.449
However, in order to create a more complete
sensorial experience, we also have to simulate

00:00:20.449 --> 00:00:22.619
how these things sound.

00:00:22.619 --> 00:00:26.820
And today, we're going to have a look at a
really cool piece of work that simulates the

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:33.400
sound of virtual elastic rods made of aluminum,
steel, oak tree, and rubber.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:38.520
And of course, before you ask, this also means
that there will be sound simulations of everyone's

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:44.510
favorite toy - the walking slinky.

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:48.800
As for all papers that have anything to do
with sound synthesis, I recommend using a

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:50.620
pair of headphones for this episode.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:55.880
The sound emerging from these elastic rods
is particularly difficult to simulate because

00:00:55.880 --> 00:01:01.460
of the fact that sound frequencies vary quite
a bit over time, and the objects themselves

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:05.750
are also in motion and subject to deformations
during the simulation.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:10.880
And as you will see with the slinky, we potentially
have tens of thousands of contact events in

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:11.920
the meantime.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:30.270
Let's have a look at some results!

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:35.180
For the Fellow Scholars who are worried about
the validity of these Star Wars sounds, I

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:38.700
know you're out there, make sure to watch
the video until the end.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:43.250
The authors of the paper proposed a dipole
model to create these simulations.

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:48.249
Dipoles are typically used to approximate
electric and magnetic fields in physics, and

00:01:48.249 --> 00:01:53.090
in this case, it is really amazing to see
an application of it for sound synthesis.

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:58.889
For instance, in most cases, these sound waves
are typically symmetric around 2D cross sections

00:01:58.889 --> 00:02:03.009
of these objects, which can be described by
a dipole model quite well.

00:02:03.009 --> 00:02:08.260
Also, it is computationally quite effective
and can eliminate these lengthy pre-computation

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:11.760
steps that are typically present in previous
techniques.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:16.700
There are also comparisons against the state
of the art, and we can hear how much richer

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:32.610
the sound of this new technique is.

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:37.500
And as you know all too well, I love all papers
that have something to do with the real world

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:38.500
around us.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:42.750
And the reason for this is that we can try
the very best kind of validation for these

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:51.700
algorithms - and this is, when we let reality
be our judge.

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:56.340
Some frequency plots are also available to
validate the output of the algorithm against

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.520
the real-world sound samples from the lab.

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:03.980
It is really amazing to see that we can use
science to breathe more life in our virtual

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:04.980
worlds.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:08.800
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

